# The ledger of `graphql`: what the GraphQL libraries of Go, Rust and Python
# let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. None of
# the three standard libraries speaks GraphQL, so the yardstick is the census
# the author chose on 2026-11-17, measured on this machine on 2026-11-18: Go's
# `github.com/graphql-go/graphql` v0.8.1, `github.com/99designs/gqlgen`
# v0.17.98 and the parser and validator it stands on,
# `github.com/vektah/gqlparser/v2` v2.5.64 (`go doc -all`, the modules
# fetched into the scratch folder's module cache); Python's `graphql-core`
# 3.3.2 and `strawberry-graphql` 0.341.2 (`dir()`, installed into the scratch
# folder with pip); Rust's `async-graphql` and `juniper`, their items read
# from docs.rs's list of each crate (no crate is built on this machine).
# graphql-js 16.15.0, installed into the scratch folder with npm, is the
# reference: every message and every answer the tests hold was asked of it.

capability document/parse  a document's text read into operations, fragments and the definitions of a schema
  heroes  graphql.parse, graphql.Document, graphql.Limits
  go      graphql-go parser.Parse, parser.ParseParams, parser.ParseOptions, parser.Parser, gqlparser.LoadQuery, gqlparser.MustLoadQuery, gqlparser parser.ParseQuery
  rust    async-graphql Request (its query parsed on execution), juniper parser::Token, parser::LexerError, parser::ParseError, parser::Spanning, parser::Span, parser::SourcePosition, parser::ScalarToken, parser::StringLiteral
  python  graphql.parse, graphql.Source, graphql.Lexer, graphql.Token, graphql.TokenKind, graphql.GraphQLSyntaxError, graphql.Location, graphql.SourceLocation, graphql.get_location, graphql.print_location, graphql.print_source_location
  note    Messages and places are graphql-js 16.15's, checked against its parser on 75 texts; descriptions on operations, fragments and variables are read as 16.15 reads them. A failure is one `invalid` whose message ends with its line and column; a request's failure reaches the response as an error with `locations`

capability document/limits  a document refused past limits the program states: bytes, tokens, nesting, aliases, directives, fields selected
  heroes  graphql.Limits, graphql.default_limits
  go      gqlparser parser.ParseQueryWithTokenLimit, gqlgen handler.Server.SetParserTokenLimit, gqlgen extension.ComplexityLimit, extension.FixedComplexityLimit, extension.ComplexityStats, extension.GetComplexityStats, gqlgen complexity.Calculate, complexity.Option, complexity.WithFixedScalarValue, complexity.WithIgnoreFields
  rust    async-graphql SchemaBuilder.limit_depth, limit_complexity, limit_recursive_depth, limit_directives, extensions::Analyzer
  python  graphql.parse(max_tokens=), graphql.validate(max_errors=), graphql.MaxIntrospectionDepthRule
  note    Bytes, tokens (graphql-js's `maxTokens` and its message), levels, aliases, directives on one place and fields selected with every fragment spread followed, counted once a fragment and saturated, so a document of fragments spread into each other is refused before the quadratic rule runs; none of the three libraries bounds the expanded count by default, gqlgen's complexity and async-graphql's limits are the nearest

capability document/format  a document written back as text
  heroes  graphql.format
  go      graphql-go printer.Print, gqlparser formatter.NewFormatter, formatter.Formatter, formatter.FormatterOption, formatter.WithIndent, formatter.WithComments, formatter.WithCompacted, formatter.WithoutDescription, formatter.WithBuiltin, formatter.WithNonIntrospectionBuiltin
  rust    -
  python  graphql.print_ast
  note    graphql-js's `print`, byte for byte on the probed documents: block strings, a field's arguments broken past 80 characters, SDL definitions

capability document/strip  a document's text with every ignored character a parser may lose left out
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  graphql.strip_ignored_characters
  note    graphql-js's `stripIgnoredCharacters` keeps exactly the separators a lexer needs between two tokens and rewrites block strings; not written in this round

capability document/operations  the operations a document holds, their kinds, names and variables
  heroes  graphql.operations_of, graphql.Operation, graphql.Variable
  go      gqlparser ast.QueryDocument.Operations, ast.OperationList.ForName
  rust    juniper OperationType, Operation
  python  graphql.get_operation_ast, graphql.OperationType

capability document/tree  the document's tree read and rewritten by a visitor
  heroes  refused: the tree is the package's own value, read by its functions; a visitor needs closures carrying the visitor's state, which the language refuses, and `operations_of`, `format` and `selected_of` answer what programs read of a document
  go      graphql-go language/ast (every node type), gqlparser ast (every node type), gqlparser validator.Walk, validator.Walker, validator.Events
  rust    juniper Selection, LookAheadSelection
  python  graphql.visit, graphql.Visitor, graphql.ParallelVisitor, graphql.TypeInfo, graphql.TypeInfoVisitor, graphql.VisitorAction, graphql.VisitorKeyMap, graphql.BREAK, graphql.SKIP, graphql.REMOVE, graphql.IDLE, graphql.Node, every `*Node` class of `graphql.language.ast`, graphql.ast_to_dict, graphql.concat_ast, graphql.separate_operations, graphql.is_definition_node, is_executable_definition_node, is_selection_node, is_value_node, is_const_value_node, is_type_node, is_type_system_definition_node, is_type_definition_node, is_type_system_extension_node, is_type_extension_node, is_subscription_operation_definition_node, is_schema_coordinate_node

capability schema/parse  a schema read from the schema definition language and checked by the type system's rules
  heroes  graphql.parse_schema, graphql.Schema
  go      gqlparser.LoadSchema, gqlparser.MustLoadSchema, gqlparser parser.ParseSchema, parser.ParseSchemas, parser.ParseSchemaWithLimit, parser.ParseSchemasWithLimit, gqlparser validator.LoadSchema, validator.ValidateSchemaDocument, validator.PreludeDocument
  rust    async-graphql dynamic::Schema (from parts), juniper RootNode
  python  graphql.build_schema, graphql.build_ast_schema, graphql.validate_schema, graphql.assert_valid_schema, graphql.LoneSchemaDefinitionRule, UniqueOperationTypesRule, UniqueTypeNamesRule, UniqueEnumValueNamesRule, UniqueFieldDefinitionNamesRule, UniqueArgumentDefinitionNamesRule, UniqueDirectiveNamesRule, PossibleTypeExtensionsRule, graphql.SDLValidationRule, graphql.specified_scalar_types, graphql.specified_directives, graphql.GraphQLInt, GraphQLFloat, GraphQLString, GraphQLBoolean, GraphQLID, GraphQLIncludeDirective, GraphQLSkipDirective, GraphQLDeprecatedDirective, GraphQLSpecifiedByDirective, GraphQLOneOfDirective, GRAPHQL_MAX_INT, GRAPHQL_MIN_INT, DEFAULT_DEPRECATION_REASON, graphql.is_specified_scalar_type, is_specified_directive
  note    graphql-js's `buildSchema` and then `validateSchema`: a schema that breaks a rule is refused at once, where graphql-js builds it and refuses it on first use. The SDL rules' messages match graphql-js on the 189 SDL cases of its test suite that do not start from an invalid schema

capability schema/extend  a schema extended with new types and `extend` definitions
  heroes  graphql.extend_schema
