capability validation/all  a document checked against a schema by every rule of the specification's section 5
  heroes  graphql.validate, graphql.rule_names
  go      graphql-go ValidateDocument, ValidationResult, ValidationContext, VisitUsingRules, the 24 `*Rule` functions, CycleErrorMessage, MisplaceDirectiveMessage, UndefinedFieldMessage, UndefinedVarMessage, UnusedVariableMessage, gqlparser validator.Validate, validator.ValidateWithSources
  rust    async-graphql ValidationMode, ValidationResult, juniper validation::RuleError, ValidatedOperation
  python  graphql.validate, graphql.specified_rules, graphql.recommended_rules, graphql.ValidationContext, graphql.ValidationRule, graphql.ASTValidationRule, every `*Rule` of section 5
  note    graphql-js 16.15's 27 `specifiedRules`, run together in its visitor's order with its messages: 529 of its 556 validation test cases agree whole with every rule run, and the 27 others start from a schema that is itself invalid, which `parse_schema` refuses (the test cases read as data from graphql-js's repository, fetched into the scratch folder). One departure, for robustness: past 100 fields under one response key, fields written alike on one type are compared once, so ten thousand copies of a field cost one comparison where graphql-js's pairwise comparison costs fifty million; below 100 the comparisons are graphql-js's pair by pair

capability validation/choose  a document checked by some rules alone
  heroes  graphql.validate_with, graphql.rule_names
  go      gqlparser validator.ValidateWithRules, validator.ValidateWithRulesWithSources, validator.AddRule, validator.RemoveRule, validator.ReplaceRule, validator.Rule, gqlgen handler.Server.SetValidationRulesFn
  rust    -
  python  graphql.validate(rules=), graphql.NoDeprecatedCustomRule, graphql.NoSchemaIntrospectionCustomRule
  note    447 of the 450 one-rule test cases agree; the three others build a schema or a document as values (a scalar whose literal parser throws, an empty selection set) that no text writes

capability validation/custom  a rule the program writes, run among the specification's
  heroes  refused: a rule of the program's own needs a visitor with closures carrying its state; a program checks what it needs on the document before `start_document`, or chooses among the rules with `validate_with`
  go      graphql-go ValidationRuleFn, ValidationRuleInstance, gqlparser validator.RuleFunc, validator.AddErrFunc, validator.ErrorOption
  rust    async-graphql CustomValidator
  python  graphql.ValidationRule subclasses, graphql.ValidationContext.report_error

capability execute/run  an operation executed, the program answering each field
  heroes  graphql.start, graphql.start_document, graphql.next_step, graphql.answer, graphql.execute, graphql.execute_with, graphql.Execution, graphql.Field, graphql.Step
  go      graphql-go Do, Execute, Params, ExecuteParams, Result, Result.HasErrors, FieldResolveFn, ResolveParams, ResolveInfo, FieldResolver, ResponsePath, gqlgen graphql.ExecutableSchema, Resolver, ResolveField, FieldContext, GetFieldContext, PathContext, GetPath, OperationContext, GetOperationContext
  rust    async-graphql Schema.execute, Request, Response, Context, Executor, juniper execute, execute_sync, ExecutionOutput, Executor, Arguments, Variables
  python  graphql.graphql, graphql.graphql_sync, graphql.execute, graphql.execute_sync, graphql.ExecutionResult, graphql.FormattedExecutionResult, graphql.GraphQLResolveInfo, graphql.GraphQLFieldResolver, graphql.ResponsePath, graphql.Executor, graphql.execution, strawberry.Info
  note    the executor hands out each field the program must resolve and takes its answer back, since no resolver can carry the program's state; a query's fields a level at a time, a mutation's root fields one at a time. 25 of 26 probed executions answer byte for byte as graphql-js does over the same data; the 26th hands a field the parent lacks to the program where graphql-js answers null

